Compilation and Execution:

    This application requires that the file structure is as follows:
    A3
     - bin
     - build
     AIPlayer.cpp
     AIPlayer.h
     Assignment_Report.txt
     game.cpp
     Makefile
     RandomPlayer.h

    Run the following commands to execute the connect 4 game:

    make clean
    make run
    ./bin/run

Minimax and Alpha-Beta pruning algorithm:

    The minimax algorithm is implemented as a member function within the Player class of 
    AIPlayer.cpp. The function is named minimax(). This function includes Alpha-Beta pruning by 
    adding the parameters alpha and beta to the minimax parameter list. Once the algorithm makes 
    it to the assignment specified depth of 6 ply, the leaf nodes are evaluated by the 
    evaluation() function and are bubbled up according to minimax plus alpha beta algorithm 
    functionality. i.e. The max player will allways pick the highest evaluated move, the min 
    player will allways pick the lowest evaluated move, and the alpha-beta pruning will prune 
    off any states where the evaluation wont matter, because the maximizer or minimizer wouldnt 
    pick it anyways.

Evaluation function:

    My evaluation function is named evaluation(), takes a state (a board) as a parameter, and
    can be found as a member funcion of the Player class in Player.cpp.
    
    It searches the entire board and for every AI marble (myMarble) it finds, it checks to see 
    if there is a row or column of 3 marbles from the other player (otherMarble). While this 
    isnt a perfect method, the intent is for the AI agent to give a high evaluation to the moves
    that are most likely blocking a connect 4 from the other player. It should be noted
    that the AI agent currently does not highly prioritize a diagonal connect 4 block.
    It also looks for states with 2 adjacent myMarbles and adds more to the evaluation score
    if those are found. For each adjacency check, if there arent two myMarbles next to eachother
    in that direction, it will subtract 1 from the evaluation score favouring the otherPlayer.
    The intent of this is to prioritize moves that are next to other myMarbles and to discourage
    moves that arent next to another myMarble.
    Finally it checks to see if there are any adjacent otherMarbles (other players marbles)
    and subtracts evaluation points to favour the other player. This final part is not 
    necessarily logical, however It was left over from a previous version of my evaluation
    function and my AI works well with it left in. 

    Overall I have extensively revised my evaluation function and I find that my AI wins against
    human and random players most of the time. Therefore I am fairly confident with the logic.

    If I had time, I would add checks for diagonal lines of 3 and evaluate the moves
    that block those lines highly.

Known Bugs:
    I do not know of any bugs in this code.

Comments:

    You can choose to play against the AI or have the AI play against the Random player you 
    provided us with by entering 1 or 2 respectively when prompted.